"A transactional outbox diagram ties a database change and an outgoing message record to one local transaction before asynchronous publication.\n\nUse when: Review reliable event publication after a local write. Explain why message delivery can repeat and consumers need idempotency.\n\nAvoid when: The database and broker already share the required supported atomic transaction.\n\nVisual grammar: A local transaction boundary encloses business data and outbox writes. A relay path publishes recorded messages to a broker. Acknowledgment or progress markers distinguish recorded, published, and consumed states.\n\nBuild: Identify the dual-write failure the design addresses. Place business update and outbox insert inside one verified transaction boundary. Draw the relay and its publication-progress mechanism. Route retry paths outside the transaction box and keep message identity visible.\n\nCheck: The relay cannot publish an uncommitted message as final business truth. Crash windows and duplicate publication remain explicit. Consumer side effects use a defined duplicate-handling strategy. Transaction borders do not cross operation or record labels.\n\nPitfalls: Claiming that an outbox alone gives end-to-end exactly-once effects. Deleting outbox entries before durable publication progress is established.\n\nExample: Order creation commits an OrderPlaced outbox record; a relay publishes it and retries safely after interruption.\n\nAlternatives: Change-data-capture diagram, Publish-subscribe diagram."
